net: cut-through forwarding for bgmac, and the cache work that pays for it

Northstar routes at gigabit only if the CPU stays out of the way. This adds
a hook that lets a module forward a frame straight from a driver's receive
path, and then removes most of the cache maintenance that the resulting
traffic pattern no longer needs.

On a Netgear R7000 the result is 945 Mbit/s both directions on *one* core,
where the software flowtable managed about 920 and needed both cores plus
packet steering.

Deliberately one patch. It was developed as four, but each later one reworked
regions the earlier ones had added - the transmit window is defined in terms
of a constant a later change moved, and the receive path was restructured
twice - so reading any single earlier patch gives an accurate picture of a
tree that no longer exists. What ships is the combination; this is it.

  1. The hook (include/linux/netdev_cutthrough.h, net/core/cutthrough.c)

Drivers offer received frames with netdev_cutthrough_rx() before
eth_type_trans(), GRO, RPS and the rest of the receive path, and forward
ndo_setup_tc() so the netfilter flowtable's hardware-offload requests reach
the module. It lives in net/core rather than in bgmac because two receive
paths feed it - bgmac's NAPI poll and Broadcom's wl driver - and a built-in
home means neither has to be loaded before the other. With nothing
registered every entry point is a NULL check.

  2. Receive: sync only the headers (bgmac)

Syncing a receive buffer for the CPU is a cache operation per 32-byte line on
this non-coherent bus, and profiling said it was where the router's CPU
actually went: at line rate, v7_dma_inv_range, v7_dma_clean_range and the
l2c210 equivalents were about 57% of the busy core, against 5% for the
forwarding code that work exists to serve.

Almost none of it was needed. A forwarded frame's payload is moved by DMA and
never read by the CPU; a frame going up the stack needs its headers read
before anything decides what to do with it. So sync BGMAC_RX_SYNC_HEAD bytes
up front and keep the mapping live until the frame's fate is known. A frame
handed to the stack has the rest synced first, and only as far as the frame
extends. Broadcom's own driver did this with a 320-byte window (CTFMAPSZ).

  3. Transmit: clean only what was rewritten (sk_buff bit + bgmac)

A frame a cut-through forwarder took arrived by DMA and had only its headers
rewritten, so only those are dirty. One sk_buff bit says so - it packs into
an existing bitfield cluster, so sk_buff does not grow - and bgmac maps such
frames without the implicit clean, writing back just the head. The bounds
that make the claim meaningful live beside the bit in netdev_cutthrough.h, so
the transmit and receive sides cannot drift apart.

  4. Receive buffer pool (bgmac + skb_pp_recycle)

What was left was the refill: a fresh fragment per frame, mapped - and
because a recycled page may hold dirty lines from anything, that map
invalidated all 1566 bytes - then unmapped again. Feeding the ring from a
page_pool recycles pages and keeps them mapped, so the per-packet map and
unmap go with it. What replaces them is a sync when a buffer is recycled, and
the freeing side gets to say how much: skb_pp_recycle() honours the
header-only bit and syncs NETDEV_CUTTHROUGH_DIRTY_LEN instead of the whole
buffer. Anything past that is left stale rather than dirty, which the device
does not care about - the driver invalidates what it is about to read.

Two details that had to be right: the DMA-stall poison is written *after* the
pool has synced the page for the device, so it needs one cache line written
back explicitly or it sits dirty over memory the MAC is about to write; and
it is one buffer per page, because the pool's sync offset is a property of
the pool and a second buffer in the same page would be synced at the wrong
one.

  Measured, wired at line rate with the load pinned

Turning the three switches off in turn, cache maintenance is 22.0% of CPU0
with all of them on and 60.1% with none, cpu0 idle 79.0% against 53.5%, and
the ordering is monotonic across all four combinations. The all-off corner
reproduces the profile from before any of this work (v7_dma_inv_range 27.5%
against the 26.8% recorded then), which is a good check that the switches
really do revert to baseline behaviour.

The part a user sees: with all of it off the router cannot hold line rate on
upload - 888 Mbit/s against 945.

  Runtime switches, so this can be measured on one image

  /sys/module/bgmac/parameters/rx_partial_sync   receive head-only sync
  /sys/module/bgmac/parameters/tx_partial_sync   transmit head-only clean
  /sys/module/bgmac/parameters/rx_page_pool      page pool (takes effect on
                                                 ring refill: ifdown/ifup, or
                                                 bgmac.rx_page_pool=0 at boot)

All default on, and rx_page_pool falls back to the page-fragment allocator by
itself if the pool cannot be created. The forwarding module's own
ctf.hdr_only turns off the header-only claim - and with it both the transmit
clean and the small recycle sync - without a rebuild.

